<h2>India's largest onion market — and its most exposed</h2>
<p>Bangladesh takes more Indian red onion than any other country we ship to, roughly USD 187 million in 2024. That scale comes directly from geography: nowhere else can Indian onion reach a major market this fast or this cheaply, whether by sea to Chattogram or overland through the West Bengal land ports.</p>

<p>The flip side of that proximity advantage is real exposure. When India has introduced a Minimum Export Price, an export duty, or — as happened for roughly six months in 2023–24 — an outright export ban, Bangladesh has felt it faster and more sharply than any other market, because there's so little transit-time buffer built into a supply chain this short. If you're building a recurring Bangladesh onion programme, this is the single most important dynamic to plan around, more than for any other market we serve.</p>

<h2>Choosing a route: Chattogram or a land border</h2>
<p>Sea freight to Chattogram (2–5 days) is the practical choice for full-container volumes, particularly onion sourced from Nashik and Maharashtra. Land-port routing through Petrapole–Benapole (1–3 days) can be faster and sometimes cheaper for smaller or more time-sensitive orders, especially if sourcing can be shifted closer to the eastern border. We don't default to one — tell us your volume, timing and sourcing region and we'll recommend the route.</p>

<h2>Documentation: SAFTA and BSTI, on top of the standard package</h2>
<p>Beyond the phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin every onion shipment needs, Bangladesh-bound orders can benefit from a SAFTA certificate of origin where preferential tariff treatment applies, and some product categories require BSTI conformity documentation. Your customs broker can confirm eligibility; we provide the underlying documentation to support it.</p>

<h2>If policy volatility is a real concern for your programme</h2>
<p>Given how directly Bangladesh feels Indian onion policy shifts, it's genuinely worth a direct conversation with us about structuring a recurring order to absorb that variability — whether that's flexible volume commitments, timing adjustments, or considering <a href="/products/oilseeds/dehydrated-onion/">dehydrated onion</a> as a more policy-stable complement to fresh-form supply.</p>

